<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Couple&nbsp;of&nbsp;potential&nbsp;nitpicks</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>all the mathematics that’s been discovered since your time</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is &quot;discovered&quot; the correct phrase? Perhaps something like &quot;all the progress in mathematics that has occurred&quot; or something similar would be more appropriate.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Did you want to talk about it?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Would &quot;do&quot; be more appropriate than &quot;did&quot;? &quot;Did&quot; seems to imply that Euclid asked for the interview.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Actually, I have an idea.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To me, this seems a bit unprofessional. Maybe something like &quot;We may actually be able to help you&quot; would be better. This might just be personal preference.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I feel the idea behind this article is quite interesting and, even in its current form, I do like the article enough to upvote.</p>
<p>However, I also feel that currently, the article is perhaps a bit &quot;bare-bones&quot;, which causes it to feel somewhat rushed and diminishes the potential impact of the story you are trying to tell. Perhaps you could add another interview ahead of the current one, set directly after the discovery of Euclid. This could allow you to establish Euclid better as a character, which could increase the impact of the second interview.</p>
<p>These are, of course, just my thoughts and more experienced users might be able to go more in-depth.</p>

							</div>

								
					
