<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;collapse</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Your first question is actually something that’s still being debated even today. I’m personally in the “math is discovered” camp rather than “math is invented”, with the caveat that you can invent explanations or proofs of mathematics, just not the actual underlying mathematical truths themselves.</p>
<p>As for the second bit, yeah I suppose that does work better. I’ll make the edit, thanks!</p>
<p>And I see where you’re coming from for the third part. I’ll change it a bit so it’s more professional than “I have an idea”.</p>
<p>I originally did plan on adding an interview immediately after his initial containment, but then realized I had no idea how to write a dialogue that occurred in Ancient Greek between a native speaker and a non-native one that was then translated into English. Plus, I was worried I wouldn’t be able to fully explain everything well, as I figured Euclid would have a lot of questions, considering he just got thrown over 2000 years into the future. I do agree though that Euclid would benefit from some more character development, so I’ll think about some ways I could do that.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Thanks for your critique!</p>

