			<div class="content" id="post-content-4290129">
									

<p>I think I will read it when I have spare time since it looks somewhat promising yet struggling to survive on the site.</p>
<p>At this moment I have one little advice: call <em>it</em> a man, not a human, if you are going to mention <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">it</span> him by the word &quot;himself&quot; in the same sentence you're introducing him.</p>
<p>It does not matter that much, but on this site, I think, it is fine to describe the person as a person, not a thing. So there is no need to call <em>him</em> an entity etc. The author chooses one — basically what I mean.</p>
<p>25 Jun 2019<br />
To be honest, I'm mildly disappointed by SCP-4721's description of the main anomaly. It's just&#8230; there. Time travel with no events. But I can understand that, it's hard to expand at this phenomena without harming the idea to show how close he is to being released and how reasonable it would be.</p>
<p>But the story is more about how hard it is to climb the mountain you've used to climb before when its top is now always there to block the sun. +1 as a token of appreciation of competent writing.</p>
<p>Still, what we have here is just one normal person that might have come from the past, but there is no strong indication of that. The article goes through claims as if they're proofs of the anomaly. I'm not feeling like I'm reading an SCP, that might be the case for dudes who downvote. Just sharing my analysis.</p>

							</div>

								
