			<div class="content" id="post-content-4290264">
									

<p>The bit where Euclid is referred to both as “the entity” and as “he/him/himself” is partially intentional. The parts referencing him as a person are supposed to be written by Dr. Krain, such as the note after the first interview and the last paragraph of the document, while the sections that would not be written by Dr. Krain, such as the containment procedures, use the term “entity”. That was a halfterthought though, so I could make it more obvious by more clearly sectioning it, such as making the description either fully use “the entity” or fully use pronouns.</p>
<p>And I suppose explicitly stating Euclid is a male would be better than just implying it with “himself”, so I’ll probably change “human” to “male human”, since that’s a bit more professional than just “man”. Thanks for your advice!</p>
<p>Edit: Yeah, I’ve gotten similar feedback about how the scip could potentially just be a normal person from our time. I put in that statement about “temporal analysis”, but I suppose that is pretty vague and doesn’t really help support the idea of Euclid being from the distant past. I had an idea that this “analysis” was something along the lines of how we can differentiate between steel made after the first nuclear weapons test and steel made before (including steel from ships that sunk before these tests), as the metal made after has small traces of radiation. While that doesn’t really apply in this scenario, I did just think of something that could be used in place of it, so I might add that in soon.</p>
<p>And you are completely right about why I didn’t elaborate on the time travel. Although I suppose it might be beneficial to add in a blurb about how the Foundation has yet to find an explanation for Euclid’s temporal displacement. Anyways, thanks again for your feedback!</p>

