			<div class="content" id="post-content-4291332">
									

<p>I've chosen to give this a review as a member of the Site Criticism Team since it's been middling between negative and positive rating for a little while.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+[Critique]&nbsp;Click&nbsp;to&nbsp;Access</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-[Click&nbsp;to&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;Text]</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Not much to comment on in regards to the containment procedures here. Concise, leaves enough to the imagination. Nothing too unusual.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>The identity of SCP-4721 was suspected after Dr. Krain, the first researcher to initiate contact, recognized that the entity was attempting to communicate through tapping the sequence of prime numbers.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's a rather specific thing to notice. If it were me, I wouldn't know it from any other kind of arrhythmic tapping, especially since it gets a little hazy after thirteen. Even then, just about anybody could be doing this. It's a bit too coincidental on top of a huge leap of logic.</p>
<hr />
<p>This interview is kind of fun. I'm not sure if you needed an interview to get this across, but it does give us a bit of personality and conflict with 4721. I feel that there is more to 4721's interests than mathematics, though. I mean, it's been two thousand years. Surely he had other interests than math?</p>
<hr />
<p>So, we're seeing the conflict realized in this second interview. I'm still a bit baffled that Euclid could have dedicated his entire life and desires to mathematics. It just barely breaks my suspension of disbelief. But I get it. He's the founder. Of course he'd be this invested and frustrated by the advances made.</p>
<p>However, I'm a little let-down by the end of the article. It seems like so easy of a solution, all without a build-up that has any real consequences. So what if Euclid is frustrated by his inability of advanced mathematics? Why should the Foundation care? I guess that's brought out by the O5 vote and the Ethics vote, but it just doesn't feel like it's rewarding to me.</p>
<hr />
<p>All-in-all, I'm not finding myself to be entertained by this. The entity is only anomalous by their nature of being from a different time, and the acclimation into the modern era is unsuccessful, so much so that they just give in and amnesticize him. I don't feel like anything was particularly lost or gained here.</p>
<p>This is a downvote from me. There isn't anything I find remarkable about this piece.</p>
</div>
</div>
