																		Re: Site Criticism Team - Junior Staff Review
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/grimmcreeper" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2843467); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2843467&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200949" alt="GrimmCreeper" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2843467)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/grimmcreeper" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2843467); return false;" >GrimmCreeper</a></span> <span class="odate time_1561612566 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Jun 2019 05:16</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4292286">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;collapse</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>On the bit about the prime numbers, you do make a good point about it being unwieldy. I suppose I can change it to just him repeating the first four prime numbers, since those are about as much as can be easily recognized. And Euclid spent quite a bit of time on prime numbers, even being credited with the proof of infinite primes by multiplying a set of primes and adding one to find a new prime (or product of new primes). That's also why it's just a suspicion, and partly why the researcher asks Euclid who he is instead of asking if he is in fact Euclid.</p>
<p>Part of the reason I wrote Euclid to be so interested in mathematics is that, besides the fact that Euclid did dedicate a significant amount of his time to learning, discovering, and writing about math, it would also be something that he would want to see where it went. Learning that his results were expanded and improved upon, such as how the converse of a theorem he proved was later also proven 1,000 years after him, would definitely have been interesting for him. And there's also the fact that we don't have a lot of information about Euclid's personal life, besides his math writings.</p>
<p>I see what you're saying about the ending not having any buildup. I originally strayed away from having a transcript of the O5 discussion included in the article, but I suppose that would be a good way to actually attach weight to the decision. In my mind, the fact that Euclid did end up being released was fairly monumental, at least in the sense of anomalous human rights. I viewed it as a demonstration of the conflict between parts of the O5 that won't neutralize an anomaly at all if they can help it and parts that view the containment of anomalous humans that could safely and easily have their properties nullified as unethical. I can completely understand how this wasn't well communicated, however, especially since I reduced this conflict and resolution into one paragraph at the end.</p>
<p>Anyways, thanks again for critiquing this article!</p>
</div>
</div>
